//! AAASM-5352 — evidence that the canonical finding model changes nothing the
//! scanner observably does.
//!
//! ADR 0032 §2 allows the canonical model to wrap `CredentialKind` and forbids
//! it from replacing, renaming or renumbering it. "Nothing observable changed"
//! is a claim about output, so this file demonstrates it with output rather
//! than asserting it in prose.
//!
//! The golden beside this file was generated by running [`render_report`] —
//! character-for-character the function below — against `main` at `95dbafe7`,
//! the commit this branch is based on, *before* any of it existed. The first
//! test regenerates the report from the current tree and requires it to be
//! byte-identical. Any change to which findings are produced, where their spans
//! start, what they are labelled, or what the redacted text looks like fails it.
//!
//! Note the direction: the golden is a *baseline capture*, not a snapshot of
//! current behaviour. It records what the scanner did before this change, so
//! regenerating it to make a failure go away would destroy the only thing it is
//! for (ADR 0032 forbidden design #9, applied to a golden of our own).
//!
//! The second test closes the other half. Byte-identical scanner output would
//! also be produced by a canonical model that simply lost information, so it
//! additionally requires every finding's kind, offset and redaction label to be
//! recoverable from its canonical form alone.

use aa_security::canonical::CanonicalFinding;
use aa_security::{CredentialScanner, ScanResult};

